==English==

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|un-|countable|id1=negative}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ʌnˈkaʊntəbəl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-uncountable.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# So [[many]] as to be incapable of being [[count]]ed.
#: {{ux|en|The reasons for our failure were as '''uncountable''' as the grains of sand on a beach.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Douglas Martin|title=About New York; Tasting History Through Papers That Age Well|magazine=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1988/07/13/nyregion/about-new-york-tasting-history-through-papers-that-age-well.html|date=1988-07-13|passage=Mr. Chambre has since spent '''uncountable''' hours and even more '''uncountable''' dollars buying upward of 23,000 documents, and he figures to be just hitting speed.}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} Incapable of being put into one-to-one [[correspondence]] with the natural [[number]]s or any subset thereof.
#: {{ux|en|Cantor’s “diagonal proof” shows that the set of real numbers is '''uncountable'''.}}
# {{lb|en|grammar|of a noun}} That cannot be used freely with numbers or the [[indefinite article]], and therefore [[:Appendix:Glossary#uncountable|usually]] takes no [[plural]] form. Example: ''[[information]]''.
#: {{ux|en|Many languages do not distinguish countable nouns from '''uncountable''' nouns.}}
#: {{ux|en|One meaning in law of the usually '''uncountable''' noun "information" is used in the plural and is countable.}}

